    "analysis": "# Old Calculator — Breaking an Affine Function with a Timing Side Channel\n\n**CTF:** Securinets CTF 2026  \n**Category:** Cryptography  \n**Challenge:** Old Calculator  \n**Status:** Solved — 16/16 rounds\n\n**Flag:** `Securinets{ed9a482110a5a6936482e700155f811742f97a04decfa75d3cee28bca77239c1}`\n\n## Overview\n\nThe challenge provides a JSON-based calculator with two arrays, `x` and `y`, each containing 100 elements. It supports basic arithmetic operations, including addition, multiplication, and operations involving constants.\n\nThe calculator allows one `submit` operation to reveal an 8-bit value. After submission, the server presents a random number and asks us to predict the output of a hidden mathematical function.\n\nTo obtain the flag, we must correctly answer **16 consecutive rounds**.\n\nThe solution combines three techniques:\n\n1. Algebraic reconstruction of the hidden function.\n2. Timing side-channel analysis using arbitrary-precision integer arithmetic.\n3. Adaptive group testing to recover the unknown parameters.\n\n## 1. Understanding the Hidden Function\n\nInitial experiments showed that the arrays contained pairs of inputs and outputs corresponding to a hidden function.\n\nWe tested the affine collinearity condition:\n\n`(y1 - y0)(x2 - x0) - (y2 - y0)(x1 - x0) = 0`\n\nThe expression evaluated to zero in independent tests, suggesting that the secret function has the form:\n\n**F(x) = ax + b**\n\nHere, `a` is the slope and `b` is the intercept.\n\nFurther experiments confirmed that these parameters were not constant between sessions. Separate slope probes produced values such as 6, 11, and 13.\n\nTherefore, both parameters had to be recovered for each round.\n\n## 2. The Submission Limitation\n\nThe calculator presents itself as an 8-bit system, but its actual arithmetic operations can produce extremely large integers.\n\nOnly the value selected for submission is restricted to 8 bits.\n\nWhen we attempted to submit a 9-bit value, the server returned an error and closed the connection.\n\nThis prevented us from directly submitting arbitrary expressions to inspect their values.\n\nHowever, ordinary arithmetic operations did not require submission and could still manipulate very large integers.\n\nThis behavior became the foundation of the exploit.\n\n## 3. Constructing a Slope Membership Test\n\nStarting from:\n\n`y[i] = a*x[i] + b`\n\nSubtract the first sample:\n\n`y[i] - y[0] = a*(x[i] - x[0])`\n\nDefine:\n\n`V = Σ(x[i] - x[0])²`\n\n`T = Σ(x[i] - x[0])*(y[i] - y[0])`\n\nfor `i = 1, 2, 3`.\n\nBy substitution:\n\n**T = aV**\n\nNow, for a candidate set of slopes `S`, construct:\n\n`P(S) = ∏(T - kV), for k ∈ S`\n\nAssuming `V ≠ 0`, the polynomial has a useful property:\n\n- If the correct slope belongs to `S`, then `P(S) = 0`.\n- Otherwise, `P(S) ≠ 0`.\n\nThis provides a mathematical membership test without requiring division.\n\nThe calculator can evaluate this polynomial entirely through its supported addition and multiplication operations.\n\nThe remaining problem is distinguishing zero from nonzero without using `submit`.\n\n## 4. Exploiting the Timing Side Channel\n\nLarge integer arithmetic is computationally expensive.\n\nMultiplication involving zero is fast, while repeated multiplication of large nonzero integers requires significantly more computation.\n\nWe exploited this difference to create a timing oracle.\n\nFirst, we construct a large constant:\n\n`C = 3^(2^11) = 3^2048`\n\nFor each candidate group, the solver performs three equivalent workloads:\n\n| Mode | Value | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Zero | 0 | Fast control |\n| Nonzero | 3 | Slow control |\n| Test | P(S) | Unknown result |\n\nEach value is multiplied by `C` and squared ten times.\n\nFor nonzero values, the resulting integer can reach approximately 3.3 million bits. When the expression equals zero, it remains zero regardless of the number of squaring operations.\n\nThe three workloads use the same number of calculator operations, allowing us to compare their execution times.\n\n### Timing Results\n\nA successful calibration produced:\n\n| Test | Zero | Nonzero | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Slope 0–15 | 381 ms | 840 ms | 429 ms |\n| Slope 0–7 | 425 ms | 871 ms | 1050 ms |\n\nThe first result strongly suggests that the slope belongs to 0–15.\n\nThe second suggests that it does not belong to 0–7.\n\nWe can therefore continue searching within 8–15.\n\nMost importantly, these tests do not consume the calculator's submission.\n\n## 5. Handling Timing Noise\n\nNetwork latency introduced occasional outliers, so individual timing measurements were not reliable enough.\n\nThe final solver uses an adaptive classifier.\n\nFor each candidate group, it measures the zero control, nonzero control, and actual polynomial.\n\nIt computes:\n\n`gap = nonzero_time - zero_time`\n\n`ratio = (test_time - zero_time) / gap`\n\nA measurement is considered usable only when the control gap reaches at least 220 milliseconds.\n\nThe classification rules are:\n\n- `ratio <= 0.35`: MATCH\n- `ratio >= 0.65`: MISS\n- Otherwise: RETRY\n\nThree agreeing valid measurements are required before accepting a decision, with up to nine attempts.\n\nIf the results remain ambiguous, the solver stops rather than sending a potentially incorrect answer.\n\n## 6. Recovering the Slope\n\nWith a working membership oracle, we perform binary search over candidate slopes.\n\nThe successful solver uses the range 0–15.\n\nFor each round:\n\n1. Verify that the slope belongs to the complete candidate range.\n2. Divide the candidates into two groups.\n3. Use the timing oracle to determine which group contains the slope.\n4. Repeat until only one candidate remains.\n5. Confirm that candidate with another timing test.\n\nFor example, round 16 produced:\n\n```text\n0..15   MATCH\n0..7    MISS   -> 8..15\n8..11   MATCH  -> 8..11\n8..9    MISS   -> 10..11\n10..10  MISS   -> 11\n11..11  MATCH\n```\n\nThe recovered slope was:\n\n**a = 11**\n\n## 7. Recovering the Intercept\n\nOnce the slope is known, we can calculate:\n\n**b = y[0] - a*x[0]**\n\nThis expression can be constructed directly inside the calculator.\n\nWe store it in an unused array position and perform one valid submission.\n\nThe server returns the submitted value and provides a fresh random challenge input `n`.\n\nWe can now predict the function's output:\n\n**F(n) = an + b**\n\nNo additional oracle queries are required.\n\n## 8. Final Exploitation\n\nThe solver repeats this procedure for all 16 rounds.\n\nThe final three rounds demonstrate the recovered parameters:\n\n| Round | a | b | Input n | Answer |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 14 | 8 | 15 | 162 | 1311 |\n| 15 | 4 | 4 | 70 | 284 |\n| 16 | 11 | 16 | 17 | 203 |\n\nNotice that the final output can exceed 255. The 8-bit restriction applies to the submitted intermediate value, not to the final prediction.\n\nAfter correctly answering round 16, the server returned:\n\n```text\n[+] Round 16: a=11, b=16, challenge=17, answer=203\n\n[+] FLAG RESPONSE:\n{\"flag\": \"Securinets{ed9a482110a5a6936482e700155f811742f97a04decfa75d3cee28bca77239c1}\"}\n```\n\n## Conclusion\n\nOld Calculator illustrates how restrictions on the output of a computation do not necessarily prevent information leakage.\n\nAlthough the calculator permits only a single 8-bit submission, it still exposes unrestricted integer arithmetic before that submission.\n\nBy constructing a polynomial that evaluates to zero for the correct slope, amplifying the computational cost of nonzero values, and observing execution-time differences, we recovered the hidden slope without consuming our submission.\n\nA single valid submission then revealed the intercept, allowing us to predict the hidden function for all 16 rounds.\n\nThe core vulnerability was an **arithmetic timing side channel**, exploited through algebraic group testing rather than direct value disclosure.\n\n**Final Flag:**\n\n`Securinets{ed9a482110a5a6936482e700155f811742f97a04decfa75d3cee28bca77239c1}`\n",
